Every glowcap session uses a small vocabulary from the
[OCapN specifications](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn/tree/main/draft-specifications).
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) explains how the parts work together, and the
[protocol diagrams](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/README.md)
draw the flows.

The operation and descriptor names below are checked against the Syrup codec.

# Operations

An operation (`op:...`) is a message that acts in the session. It opens the
session, makes a call, settles a promise, or tears the whole thing down.

| Message | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `op:start-session` | Opens a session. Each side announces its location and the keys that bind the connection. |
| `op:deliver` | Calls a method on a reference. Next requires a resolver and listen mode. The optional answer position enables pipelining. On 1.0, omitting both answer and resolver makes a one-way send. |
| `op:deliver-only` | Next sends a message without requesting a result. |
| `op:resolve` | Settles an answer to its value or a break. glowcap never sends it to a live peer: CapTP 1.0 settles through the resolver below, and `glowcap-next` through its inline subscription or `op:listen`. The codec reads and writes it for the reference fixtures. |
| `op:listen` | Subscribes to a promise's resolution, so a waiter is told the answer the moment it lands. |
| `op:abort` | Ends the session with a typed reason. A refused handshake is answered the same way, before the session ever opens. |
| `op:get` | On 1.0, reads a named field of an answer without waiting for the whole result. |
| `op:index` | On 1.0, reads one item of an answer by position. |
| `op:untag` | Unwraps a tagged value read from an answer. glowcap accepts it on every 1.0 session and sends it only when the partner advertises the `untag` feature, so a silent Goblins peer never sees one. |
| `op:gc-exports` | Tells the peer that some exported references are no longer held, so it can drop them. |
| `op:gc-answers` | Tells the peer that some answers are no longer needed, so it can release them. |

# [How a promise settles]($section.id('how-a-promise-settles'))

On a CapTP 1.0 session, a caller that wants an answer passes a **resolver**
with its `op:deliver`: a reference the callee calls back. The answer comes
home as one more `op:deliver`, to that resolver, carrying
`['fulfill <value>]` or `['break <error>]`. Goblins and glowcap both settle
promises this way.

`glowcap-next` tracks the draft's operation contracts while keeping the shared
Goblins-compatible hello and crypto formats. A call carries its resolver and a
listen mode inline: mode 1 requests shortening, and mode 3 requests shortening
or settlement. A separate `op:listen` can add a mode-2 settling subscription
after mode 1. Notifications use `op:deliver-only`. Next refuses `op:get`,
`op:index`, and `op:untag`. Those wire projections remain on 1.0.

# What a CapTP 1.0 peer sees from glowcap

A Goblins peer, or any other CapTP 1.0 implementation, sees plain CapTP 1.0
over Syrup. glowcap's preferences travel as three hints on the location its
hello signs: `glowcap/captp-version`, `glowcap/codec`, and `glowcap/features`.
A peer that doesn't know them ignores them, and the session uses the 1.0
baseline. The version and codec hints for glowcap's own tracks also name the
exact wire a build writes, so two glowcap builds that write it differently use
the 1.0 baseline. Goblins refuses a hinted onion address, so over Tor glowcap
sends hints only to a partner it already knows is glowcap. With anyone else, an
onion session runs the baseline.

glowcap sends `op:untag` only to a partner that lists the `untag` feature.
Application code makes the same calls under either version. The one
difference is `session.listen`, which accepts a live far object on 1.0 but
only a far promise on `glowcap-next`.

# Reference descriptors

A descriptor (`desc:...`) names a reference or a handoff *inside* a message:
what a slot points at.

| Descriptor | What it names |
| --- | --- |
| `desc:export` | A reference back to an object the receiver exports: how the sender names something it was given earlier (position 0 is the bootstrap object). |
| `desc:answer` | A reference to a pending answer from an earlier `op:deliver`, the target of promise pipelining. |
| `desc:import-object` | An object the sender hosts and is introducing. The receiver records it as an import. |
| `desc:import-promise` | A promised reference the sender is introducing, settled later when the sender resolves it. |
| `desc:handoff-give` | The gifter's half of a three-party introduction: "go meet this peer, on my word." |
| `desc:handoff-receive` | The recipient's half of that introduction, redeemed with the third party. |
| `desc:sig-envelope` | A signed wrapper that makes a handoff unforgeable as it crosses between the three parties. |

# Locators

A locator (`ocapn-...`) is neither a message nor a reference. It is an *address*:
where a peer lives, and, for a sturdyref, which object to ask it for. The
*designator* inside it is the peer's name on its transport: an iroh node id, a
public key for Noise and keyed transports, a Tor service id, or a `host:port`
for testing-only `tcp`. A locator rides inside `op:start-session` as the
location each side announces, and travels as an argument when one peer hands
another a long-lived reference to bring back to life later. The
`ocapn://` URI you paste into a config is one of these records, in text.

| Locator | What it addresses |
| --- | --- |
| `ocapn-peer` | A peer's location: its transport (`iroh`, `noise-tcp`, `tcp`, and the rest), the designator to reach it at, and any connection hints. An `ocapn://<designator>.<transport>` URI is this record. |
| `ocapn-sturdyref` | A peer location plus a swiss number: a restorable, long-lived reference to one object, the thing an `ocapn://…/s/<swiss>` URI names. |

Examples, as the URIs print. Reserved characters inside a designator are
URI-encoded, which is why a `tcp` peer's `host:port` shows a `%3A` where the
colon is. `noise-tcp` seals a direct route. Plain `tcp` is
testing-only:

```
ocapn://<64 hex chars>.iroh
ocapn://<64 hex chars>.noise-tcp?direct-addresses=127.0.0.1%3A9099
ocapn://127.0.0.1%3A46659.tcp
```

The `id` locator bundles several such routes under one durable key.
[Multi-home peers]($link.page('multihome')) covers it.

[Transports]($link.page('transports')) lists what a locator can name, and
[Codecs]($link.page('codecs')) compares the wire encodings.
